    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.prominent_group - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.prominent_group.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.prominent_group()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find the group of k nodes with the highest group betweenness.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.prominent_group()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find the group of k nodes with the highest group betweenness.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.prominent_group()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Find the group of k nodes with the highest group betweenness.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind the group of `k` nodes with the highest group betweenness.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L53752-L53891){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.prominent_group(\n    k,\n    weighted=None,\n    exclude=None,\n    endpoints=False,\n    normalized=True,\n    greedy=False,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Most prominent group](../assets/graphs/prominent-group.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The shared seven-node graph twice. Left, prominent_group(2) finds the pair E, B (accent), which together lie on 0.65 of the shortest paths between the other five nodes. Right, E and F, two of the three nodes with the highest individual betweenness, cover much of the same traffic, so as a group they score only 0.40.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\n`group_betweenness_centrality()` scores a group you choose; this method finds the best\ngroup of a given size: the `k` nodes that, together, lie on the largest share of\nshortest paths between the other nodes. The best group is usually not the `k`\nindividually most central nodes, because two hubs on the same routes intercept the same\ntraffic. Typical uses are placing `k` monitors, checkpoints or brokers so that between\nthem they see as much of the network's traffic as possible.\n\n\"Shortest\" follows `shortest_path()`: hops on an unweighted graph, total weight on a\nweighted one. Scaling is the same as in `group_betweenness_centrality()`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The group size (at least 1).\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to measure path length by edge weight. If `None` (default), follows the\n    graph.\n\n<code>[exclude]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[Union[int, str]] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Nodes that may not be chosen, as integer IDs or string labels. Paths through them\n    still count. Default is `None` (any node may be chosen).\n\n<code>[endpoints]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, also count pairs with an endpoint in the group. Default is `False`.\n\n<code>[normalized]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), divide by `(n - k)(n - k - 1)` so the score lies in `[0, 1]`.\n    NetworkX 3.7 uses the same divisor with `endpoints=True`, so EdgeCase does too,\n    although the score can then exceed 1. `group_betweenness_centrality(endpoints=True)`\n    divides by `n(n - 1)` instead, so set `normalized=False` to compare the two.\n\n<code>[greedy]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, add the node with the largest gain one at a time instead of searching\n    exhaustively. Much faster on large graphs, but the group may not be the best one.\n    Default is `False`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [tuple[float, list[int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The group betweenness of the best group and its node IDs, in the order the search\n    chose them.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `k` is less than 1 or more than the number of nodes that may be chosen, a node in\n    `exclude=` does not exist, or weights are used and one is negative.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe search is the depth-first branch and bound of Puzis, Elovici and Dolev (2007). It\nprecomputes the path betweenness of every pair of candidates (the share of shortest\npaths through both) in $O(V^3)$; adding a member updates that matrix in $O(V^2)$ to\ncount only paths avoiding the group, and a branch is pruned when its value plus the\n`k` best remaining gains cannot beat the best group so far. The search is exact; its\nresult always equals `group_betweenness_centrality()` of the group returned.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~group_betweenness_centrality`) \n\n:   Score a group that is already chosen.\n\n[](`~knotty_centrality`) \n\n:   A dense, central core of any size.\n\n[](`~voterank`) \n\n:   Choose spreaders one at a time by voting.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn two triangles joined by a path, the best pair is the two ends of the path, which\nbetween them sit on every route from one triangle to the other:\n\n::: {#04000a42 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\"),\n     (\"E\", \"F\"), (\"F\", \"G\"), (\"G\", \"E\")]\n)\n\nscore, group = graph.prominent_group(2)\nscore, [graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in group]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(0.8, ['E', 'C'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Three checkpoints on the metro\n\nOn the metro, the three stations with the most betweenness are Market, Eastfield and\nRiverside, but Eastfield and Market are next to each other on the line to the Airport,\nand as a group the three lie on 57% of the fastest routes between the other stations.\nThe best three, Market, Museum and Old Town, sit around Central to the east, north and\nwest, and between them cover 75% of the routes:\n\n::: {#59e434e1 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\n\nscore, group = metro.prominent_group(3)\n\n(\n    score,\n    [metro.get_label_from_id(n) for n in group],\n    metro.group_betweenness_centrality([\"Market\", \"Eastfield\", \"Riverside\"]),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(0.7524509803921572, ['Market', 'Museum', 'Old Town'], 0.5649509803921567)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#5b65d0ab .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro.plot(layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", highlight=group)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-